The new preparation defines period/frequency/wavelength and photon energy before spectral interpretation; distinguishes orbital/probability descriptions from classical trajectories; connects electron counting with formal charge, oxidation state and geometry; and derives the constant-pressure thermal account from an explicit boundary and sign convention. Quantum-number capacity is distinguished from many-electron filling order. Polarity illustrations use declared vector conventions without claiming measured bond or molecular dipoles. The shared advanced quantum, logarithmic phase, pressure-volume integral and full course/laboratory assignments remain available with their later prerequisites explicit.

The original study combines stipulated spectral levels and structural accounts with a fictional material-X thermal model. It does not claim that the model's invented heat capacities, fusion enthalpy or transition temperature describe water or another identified substance. The phase-energy calculation independently compares process stages with reference-state differences, treats partial melting and both thresholds, and explains how changing the boundary changes heat/work accounting. It distinguishes phase change from chemical reaction, an algebraic result from an identified material property, and a calibrated observation from a hypothetical example. No laboratory experiment or real-material validation is claimed.

